<file path="docs/adr/0001-headless-ui-for-accessible-primitives.md">
# Use Headless UI for accessible interactive primitives

Interactive primitives (starting with Tabs) are thin, Tailwind-styled wrappers around `@headlessui/react` rather than hand-rolled implementations of the WAI-ARIA patterns or another headless library such as React Aria or Radix. Headless UI is already a project dependency, it is unstyled so our tokens stay in full control, and it gives us keyboard handling, focus management and ARIA wiring we would otherwise have to write and test ourselves. Our wrappers follow the library's own API shape (for Tabs, selection by index with panels matched to tabs by order) instead of layering a different model on top, so the wrappers stay thin and the library's documentation stays applicable.
</file>

<file path="docs/adr/0001-headless-ui-for-popup-controls.md">
# Popup controls are built on Headless UI

Controls that open a popup (starting with Select) get their open/close, keyboard, focus and ARIA behaviour from `@headlessui/react`, and all styling stays ours in Tailwind. We chose this over hand-rolling the listbox pattern, which is a lot of accessibility-critical code to own, and over a native `<select>`, which can't be styled to the Horaion design. Headless UI was already a dependency and is unstyled, so it doesn't compete with the Horaion colour ranges.

## Consequences

Later popup controls (Combobox, Menu, Dialog, Popover) should reach for the matching Headless UI primitive first, so that keyboard and focus behaviour stays consistent across the product.
</file>

<file path="docs/adr/0002-hand-rolled-calendar-on-dayjs.md">
# The Calendar grid is hand-rolled on dayjs

Headless UI has no calendar primitive, so ADR 0001 doesn't cover the Calendar. We build its date grid ourselves on the WAI-ARIA date-grid pattern, with keyboard, roving focus and ARIA written and tested in-house. dayjs handles all date maths and localised month and weekday names. We rejected react-aria's `useCalendar`/`useRangeCalendar` because it is a heavy dependency and would bring a second date type (`@internationalized/date`) alongside dayjs. We rejected react-day-picker because it has no month or year views and would need restyling to fit the Horaion design.

## Consequences

We own a meaningful amount of accessibility-critical code, so the grid's keyboard and ARIA behaviour must stay thoroughly unit tested. Dates cross the public API as ISO date strings (`YYYY-MM-DD`), never as dayjs objects, so callers don't depend on dayjs. When the Date picker wraps the Calendar in a popup, that popup still uses Headless UI's Popover, as ADR 0001 requires.
</file>

<file path="docs/adr/0003-frontend-owns-user-facing-wording.md">
# The frontend owns all user-facing wording

Every word a person reads in Horaion comes from the app's translation files, so that adding a language means translating one set of files in one repo. The app never displays text the API sends: not a ProblemDetail's `detail` or `title`, not a success response's `message`, and not raw enum constants such as `PENDING_VERIFICATION`. Instead it maps the API's stable codes (`errorCode`, enum constants, HTTP status) to translation keys, with a translated fallback for each status. We rejected localising on the server (Spring `MessageSource` with `Accept-Language`) because the wording would then live in two repos, in two file formats, and the app would still need its own translations for everything else.

## Consequences

The API's English text is for developers and logs only, so ignoring it is deliberate, not an oversight. An error the app needs to explain specifically must carry a stable code. Where the API has no code yet, the app can only show a generic message for that status. Emails that Cognito sends are outside both repos and are not covered by this decision.
</file>

<file path="docs/adr/0004-sessions-through-the-api-bff.md">
# Sessions go through the API's backend-for-frontend

Status: Proposed — Moses is asked to accept this decision in the PR before merge.

Every credential exchange goes through the Horaion API's backend-for-frontend (BFF), which calls Cognito with a confidential client whose secret stays in API configuration. Horaion keeps its own screens. The API holds the Session in an HttpOnly, Secure, `SameSite=Strict` cookie scoped to the Session routes; the app holds a short-lived access pass in memory only. "Remember me" is removed and every Session lasts seven days unless ended earlier. This follows API ADR-0019, "Browser token handling — access token in memory, refresh token in an httpOnly cookie", and API ADR-0029, "People sign in on Horaion's own pages, through a backend-for-frontend in the api".

We rejected the in-browser Cognito library holding tokens in `localStorage`: a script injection could copy a long-lived refresh token and use it from another machine, and a public Cognito client bypasses the API's rate limits and audit. Moving only the refresh token into a cookie would leave that public client callable. The API already provides the BFF, so no separate service is needed. Hosted Cognito pages would leave Horaion's own Onboarding and Challenge flow.

The email travels between auth screens in tab-scoped history state, surviving reloads but never entering a URL or web storage. During Session recovery, only an expired Session sends a person to Log in; an unreachable API shows a retry screen. Removed access to a Company re-settles the Current Company and keeps the Session, including when no usable Company remains. For authentication, the last chosen Company id is the only thing in `localStorage`; it is a preference, not a credential, and is ignored when the Company or membership is inactive.

## Consequences

The app and API must share a registrable domain for `SameSite=Strict` cookies. Cookie routes require an allowed Origin and JSON bodies for CSRF protection. The API sees passwords in transit and owns credential exchange, rate limiting and audit. The ID token never reaches the browser; the person's details come from `/me`.

The shared request helper attaches the access pass to authenticated calls, renews once on a bearer 401 and retries. Renewal is single-flight within a tab and serialised across tabs with a Web Lock where available. Reloads and new tabs recover through the cookie. Log out clears the pass, query cache and pending Challenge and broadcasts to other tabs even if the API call fails; server-side revocation still requires that call to succeed. Browser tests mock these routes, rather than storing tokens. The existing language detector also caches a locale in `localStorage`; the PR records this gap against the spec's Company-id-only storage rule.
</file>

<file path="docs/adr/0006-editing-an-access-role-never-changes-what-it-cannot-show.md">
# Editing an access role never changes what it cannot show

Status: Proposed — Moses is asked to accept this decision in the PR before merge.

The system role editor sends a role's whole permission set on every save, because the API's `PUT` replaces the set. Three rules keep that save from changing authority the person did not see or could not have granted.

A permission key the role holds that is not in the app's copy of the catalog (`features/workspace/permissionKeys.ts`) cannot be shown as a checkbox. The editor keeps such keys aside and sends them back unchanged with every save. Dropping them would silently remove a permission from everyone holding the role whenever the app is a release behind the API.

A role is shown read-only when it is the Company Owner role, when the person lacks `access_role:update` at the company, or when the role holds any permission the person does not hold at the company. The last case mirrors the API's permission subset rule (API ADR-0017), which checks the union of the role's current and requested permissions and so refuses every change to such a role, a rename included. Following ADR-0005, this is a courtesy drawn from the workspace, not a control: the API still decides, and a save it refuses with a 403 is explained in the app's own words.

Ticking any permission of a resource also ticks that resource's view permission, and clearing view clears the resource. The API does not require this. It is a default that stops a role being given a change it cannot see, and it applies only to what the person ticks: a role that already holds an edit permission without view is shown as it is and is not altered by opening it.

We rejected hiding or disabling single checkboxes the person cannot grant, which lets them build a change the API will then refuse. We also rejected asking the API which permissions the person may grant: the workspace already carries their company-level keys, and the API's answer on save is the one that counts.

## Consequences

After a save the app reloads the workspace as well as the Organisation lists, because the role may be one the person holds. The API's decision engine takes a moment to pick up changed roles, so the reloaded workspace can briefly still show the old permissions.

Two people editing the same role overwrite each other, the later save winning. The API has no version check on access roles; adding one is an API decision.

The role is fetched again on every visit to the editor and is never read from a cache, so an edit always starts from the role as it is now.
</file>

<file path="docs/agents/domain.md">
# Domain Docs

How the engineering skills should consume this repo's domain documentation when exploring the codebase.

## Before exploring, read these

- **`CONTEXT.md`** at the repo root, or
- **`CONTEXT-MAP.md`** at the repo root if it exists: it points at one `CONTEXT.md` per context. Read each one relevant to the topic.
- **`docs/adr/`**: read ADRs that touch the area you're about to work in. In multi-context repos, also check `src/<context>/docs/adr/` for context-scoped decisions.

If any of these files don't exist, **proceed silently**. Don't flag their absence; don't suggest creating them upfront. The `/domain-modeling` skill (reached via `/grill-with-docs` and `/improve-codebase-architecture`) creates them lazily when terms or decisions actually get resolved.

## File structure

Single-context repo (most repos):

```
/
├── CONTEXT.md
├── docs/adr/
│   ├── 0001-event-sourced-orders.md
│   └── 0002-postgres-for-write-model.md
└── src/
```

Multi-context repo (presence of `CONTEXT-MAP.md` at the root):

```
/
├── CONTEXT-MAP.md
├── docs/adr/                          ← system-wide decisions
└── src/
    ├── ordering/
    │   ├── CONTEXT.md
    │   └── docs/adr/                  ← context-specific decisions
    └── billing/
        ├── CONTEXT.md
        └── docs/adr/
```

## Use the glossary's vocabulary

When your output names a domain concept (in an issue title, a refactor proposal, a hypothesis, a test name), use the term as defined in `CONTEXT.md`. Don't drift to synonyms the glossary explicitly avoids.

If the concept you need isn't in the glossary yet, that's a signal: either you're inventing language the project doesn't use (reconsider) or there's a real gap (note it for `/domain-modeling`).

## Flag ADR conflicts

If your output contradicts an existing ADR, surface it explicitly rather than silently overriding:

> _Contradicts ADR-0007 (event-sourced orders), but worth reopening because…_
</file>

<file path="docs/agents/issue-tracker.md">
# Issue tracker: Local Markdown

Issues and specs for this repo live as markdown files in `.scratch/`.

## Conventions

- One feature per directory: `.scratch/<feature-slug>/`
- The spec is `.scratch/<feature-slug>/spec.md`
- Implementation issues are one file per ticket at `.scratch/<feature-slug>/issues/<NN>-<slug>.md`, numbered from `01`, never a single combined tickets file
- Triage state is recorded as a `Status:` line near the top of each issue file (see `triage-labels.md` for the role strings)
- Comments and conversation history append to the bottom of the file under a `## Comments` heading

## When a skill says "publish to the issue tracker"

Create a new file under `.scratch/<feature-slug>/` (creating the directory if needed).

## When a skill says "fetch the relevant ticket"

Read the file at the referenced path. The user will normally pass the path or the issue number directly.

## Wayfinding operations

Used by `/wayfinder`. The **map** is a file with one **child** file per ticket.

- **Map**: `.scratch/<effort>/map.md` (the Notes / Decisions-so-far / Fog body).
- **Child ticket**: `.scratch/<effort>/issues/NN-<slug>.md`, numbered from `01`, with the question in the body. A `Type:` line records the ticket type (`research`/`prototype`/`grilling`/`task`); a `Status:` line records `claimed`/`resolved`.
- **Blocking**: a `Blocked by: NN, NN` line near the top. A ticket is unblocked when every file it lists is `resolved`.
- **Frontier**: scan `.scratch/<effort>/issues/` for files that are open, unblocked, and unclaimed; first by number wins.
- **Claim**: set `Status: claimed` and save before any work.
- **Resolve**: append the answer under an `## Answer` heading, set `Status: resolved`, then append a context pointer (gist + link) to the map's Decisions-so-far in `map.md`.
</file>

<file path="docs/agents/triage-labels.md">
# Triage Labels

The skills speak in terms of five canonical triage roles. This file maps those roles to the actual label strings used in this repo's issue tracker.

| Label in mattpocock/skills | Label in our tracker | Meaning                                  |
| -------------------------- | -------------------- | ---------------------------------------- |
| `needs-triage`             | `needs-triage`       | Maintainer needs to evaluate this issue  |
| `needs-info`               | `needs-info`         | Waiting on reporter for more information |
| `ready-for-agent`          | `ready-for-agent`    | Fully specified, ready for an AFK agent  |
| `ready-for-human`          | `ready-for-human`    | Requires human implementation            |
| `wontfix`                  | `wontfix`            | Will not be actioned                     |

When a skill mentions a role (e.g. "apply the AFK-ready triage label"), use the corresponding label string from this table.

Edit the right-hand column to match whatever vocabulary you actually use.
</file>

